<article class="env-proof">
    <p>
      <strong>Proof.</strong>
      It is clear that $(1)$ implies $(2)$. Let us prove the converse. Since the class of categorical equivalences is stable under filtered colimits, it suffices to consider the case where $T$ has only finitely many nondegenerate simplices. We now work by induction on the dimension of $T$, and the number of nondegenerate simplices contained in $T$. If $T$ is empty, there is nothing to prove. Otherwise, we may write $T = T' \coprod _{\bd \Delta ^ n} \Delta ^ n$. By the inductive hypothesis, the maps 
    <p>
      <div class="equation">
  \[  X_{T'} \rightarrow Y_{T'} \]
</div>
    <p>
      <div class="equation">
  \[  X_{\bd \Delta ^ n} \rightarrow Y_{\bd \Delta ^ n} \]
</div>
    <p>
       are categorical equivalences, and by assumption $X_{\Delta ^ n} \rightarrow Y_{\Delta ^ n}$ is a categorical equivalence as well. We note that 
    <p>
      <div class="equation">
  \[ X_{T} = X_{T'} \coprod _{ X_{\bd \Delta ^ n} } X_{\Delta ^ n} \]
</div>
    <p>
      <div class="equation">
  \[ Y_{T} = Y_{T'} \coprod _{ Y_{\bd \Delta ^ n} } Y_{\Delta ^ n}. \]
</div>
    <p>
       Since the Joyal model structure is left-proper, these pushouts are homotopy pushouts, and therefore categorically equivalent to one another. 
      <span class="qed">$\square$</span>
    </p>
</article>